#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""attavo: the command.

    attavo doctor [TENANT_ROOT] [--json]
    attavo spec-gate [N]
    attavo approve [N] --by LOGIN
    attavo approval-status [N]
    attavo build [N]
    attavo verify [N]
    attavo file-findings [N]
    attavo record-only
    attavo attest [--partial] [N]

The doctor is the definition of done. It asserts three proofs against a
tenant root and exits 1 until every proof holds:

  1. every-gate-rejects      every gate in the manifest's [gates] table,
                             run against its rejection fixture, exits non-zero,
                             and carries a non-empty retires
  2. one-item-every-stage    one work item under paths.records carries a
                             record for every stage -- spec, approval,
                             build, verify -- all four introduced by one
                             merge commit reachable from HEAD
  3. no-commit-lacks-record  every commit on the default branch after the
                             manifest was introduced is named by a record's
                             sha or commits list, or itself writes under
                             paths.records (a record is committed with the
                             code it describes; an item's merge commit
                             carries its own record paths this way)

Nothing in doctor is a gate. The doctor reads; it never writes and it never
blocks a merge by itself. It is what CI runs to say whether the product
is done.

spec-gate, approve, and approval-status are gates and writers. They run
from the tenant root (the current working directory); N, the work item id,
is read from the ATTAVO_ITEM environment variable when set, else from the
branch (attavo/N-*). The doctor's first proof sets ATTAVO_ITEM for a
rejection fixture's own item, so a gate's own command needs no argument
even while the enclosing repository sits on a real item's attavo/N-*
branch. Every run of spec-gate or approve writes the next numbered attempt
file under paths.records/N/ and never overwrites one already written. See
the spec contract in the project's Phase 1 issue for the full rules each
one enforces.

build refuses (exit 1, naming the cause, no record) unless the manifest
declares [executor].build and [tests].select and [tests].run, and unless
approval-status would print valid. It exports the executor's identity
(issue #33) into the adapter's environment as GIT_AUTHOR_NAME/EMAIL and
GIT_COMMITTER_NAME/EMAIL, set by the product and never the tenant's git
config, then invokes the named adapter (adapters/<name>/run) with the
spec, the corpus, and the branch. It refuses (status refused) a commit
between start_sha and HEAD not authored by that identity, checks that
every path touched matches a spec claim, records the adapter's own
declared "reports" -- the fields adapters/<name>/conformance.json says
it can supply with a real value (issue #41), so a field it claims and
never supplies is a defect the conformance suite catches rather than a
silent null -- then runs the criteria, the
tests [tests].select and [tests].run name for the touched paths, and --
when the manifest declares [checks].command -- the tenant's own
whole-tree check, from the tenant root. pass requires every criterion,
the test run, and the tenant's checks (if declared) to exit 0; any
non-zero exit iterates, with the failure as feedback, up to a fixed
budget. [checks] is optional; a tenant that declares none is not
refused. It writes the next build-NN.json under paths.records/N/, with
the adapter's transcript beside it as build-NN.log. See the build
contract in the project's Phase 2 issue for the full rules it enforces.

verify refuses (exit 1) unless the item's latest build record has
status pass, and unless the manifest declares [forge].checks_command --
a tenant command, taking a full commit sha on argv, that prints one
line per check as "<name> <conclusion>". verify runs it on the build
record's sha first: any conclusion other than success, neutral, or
skipped writes verify-NN.json with status returned and calls no
adapter, because a judge is never spent on a tree the forge already
called red. With the forge green, verify calls the adapter named by
[executor].verify (defaulting to [executor].build) with the diff since
the build's approval, the spec, and the build record, and records its
findings and its declared "reports" (issue #41). [executor.<adapter>.<stage>] shadows [executor.<adapter>] for
build or verify alone; both merge parent then stage before setting
ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG, and the record's adapter_config is the merged
result. file-findings opens one tenant issue per finding in the item's
latest verify record via [forge].file_issue_command, and refuses (exit
1, nothing filed) a record carrying a finding with no retires. See the
verify contract in the project's Phase 2 issue for the full rules both
enforce.

record-only exits 0 when HEAD, against its first parent, touches only
paths.records; 1 otherwise; 2 with no manifest. Core logic about a git
checkout, not a forge's shell -- a workflow template's push-and-retrigger
guard is one line reading this exit code instead of carrying its own
copy of the check.

attest refuses (exit 1) unless the latest spec, approval, build, and
verify records for an item all exist and chain: the build's approval_sha
names the latest approval, and the verify's build_sha names that build --
a re-approval after a build must force a re-run of what changed. With no
N given it derives the item from the record paths HEAD's merge commit
touched under paths.records/<N>/, not from a branch name, which a squash
merge discards and which names a forge's convention the product does not
know; more than one item derived is a refusal naming what it found. Zero
is not a defect -- not every merge is an item merge -- exit 0, nothing
printed but a line saying so.

attest never writes. On a coherent chain it prints the attestation
statement -- validating against schema/attestation.schema.json -- to
stdout as JSON and exits 0: the item, its four records and their shas,
and the commit the statement was computed at (sha and merge_sha are the
same commit here; on the merge group that commit is the one that lands).
The four stage records already under paths.records are the evidence for
that statement; a forge-specific template signs the printed JSON,
unmodified, as the predicate, against the commit that lands, on the merge
group -- and the signed bundle in the forge's attestation store is the
seal over it. attest itself never signs anything and never talks to a
signing service. --partial (issue #67) is for a pull request, where the
record a complete chain would need is the output of a later stage that is
itself waiting on this check: a missing later record is "not yet" (exit
0, naming the item and the stages present and absent, no statement
printed) rather than a refusal, while the two chain assertions -- build's
approval_sha names the latest approval, verify's build_sha names the
latest build -- still run over whatever records exist. merge_group and a
push to main still demand the whole chain. See issue #69's lane for the
full rules it enforces.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import fnmatch
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import tomllib
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

MANIFEST = "attavo.toml"
STAGES = ("spec", "approval", "build", "verify")
CRITERION_TIMEOUT = 120
RECORD_RE = re.compile(r"^([a-z]+)-([0-9]{2,})\.json$")
BUILD_ITERATION_BUDGET = 3
BUILD_HALT_EXIT = 3
CHECKS_FEEDBACK_TAIL_LINES = 200
PRODUCT_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent


class Proof:
    def __init__(self, key: str, title: str) -> None:
        self.key = key
        self.title = title
        self.holds = False
        self.reason = ""
        self.extra: dict = {}

    def fail(self, reason: str, **extra) -> "Proof":
        self.holds, self.reason = False, reason
        self.extra.update(extra)
        return self

    def hold(self, reason: str, **extra) -> "Proof":
        self.holds, self.reason = True, reason
        self.extra.update(extra)
        return self

    def as_dict(self) -> dict:
        return {"proof": self.key, "title": self.title,
                "holds": self.holds, "reason": self.reason, **self.extra}


def read_manifest(root: Path) -> dict | None:
    path = root / MANIFEST
    if not path.is_file():
        return None
    with path.open("rb") as fh:
        return tomllib.load(fh)


def git(root: Path, *args: str) -> str:
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), *args],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(out.stderr.strip() or "git failed")
    return out.stdout


def product_version() -> str:
    """What the running program is. Three fallbacks, in order, and never an
    exception -- this runs inside write_record, after the work it
    describes already happened, and a raise here would lose the record
    for an invocation that already ran:

      1. ATTAVO_PRODUCT_VERSION, when the environment sets it. The forge
         action sets this from its own ref (action.yml, not here -- this
         function stays forge-agnostic).
      2. git describe --tags --always --dirty against PRODUCT_ROOT: a
         tagged release reports its tag, a checkout reports its commit,
         a dirty tree says so.
      3. "unknown", when PRODUCT_ROOT is not a git repository at all --
         the case for a forge that extracts this action from a tarball
         rather than a git checkout, which is exactly when (1) is
         expected to already have supplied the answer.

    Distinct from the tenant's product_pin -- an operator running a
    checkout, which is how every build and verify have actually been run
    so far, bypasses the pin entirely (issue #49)."""
    env = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_PRODUCT_VERSION")
    if env:
        return env
    try:
        return git(PRODUCT_ROOT, "describe", "--tags", "--always", "--dirty").strip()
    except RuntimeError:
        return "unknown"


def now_iso() -> str:
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def derive_item_id(root: Path) -> str | None:
    """ATTAVO_ITEM first, not last: a rejection fixture's own item id must
    win over whatever attavo/N-* branch the enclosing tenant repository
    happens to be checked out on -- the common case is doctor running from
    a real item's own branch, where a fallback-only env var would never be
    consulted at all and every gate fixture would silently resolve to the
    real item instead of its own."""
    env_item = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_ITEM")
    if env_item:
        return env_item
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    branch = out.stdout.strip()
    m = re.match(r"^attavo/(\d+)-", branch)
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def fixture_item_id(fixture: Path) -> str | None:
    """The one item id under a rejection fixture's own paths.specs, so the
    doctor can export it as ATTAVO_ITEM: a fixture carries no attavo/N-*
    branch for a gate command with no argument to derive from otherwise."""
    fm = read_manifest(fixture)
    specs_rel = ((fm or {}).get("paths") or {}).get("specs")
    if not specs_rel:
        return None
    specs_dir = fixture / specs_rel
    if not specs_dir.is_dir():
        return None
    items = sorted(p.name for p in specs_dir.iterdir() if p.is_dir())
    return items[0] if len(items) == 1 else None


class ShallowCheckoutError(RuntimeError):
    pass


def last_commit_touching(root: Path, relpath: str) -> str | None:
    shallow = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if shallow.stdout.strip() == "true":
        raise ShallowCheckoutError(
            "shallow checkout: git log -1 -- <path> returns HEAD for every path on a "
            "shallow clone; check out with fetch-depth: 0")
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "log", "-1", "--format=%H", "--", relpath],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    sha = out.stdout.strip()
    return sha or None


# --- record files: <stage>-NN.json, one per attempt, never overwritten ---

SCHEMA_RECORDS_DIR = PRODUCT_ROOT / "schema" / "records"


def stage_schema(stage: str) -> dict:
    """schema/records/<stage>.schema.json -- this repository's own
    declaration of that stage's record shape (issue #60). The only
    artifact the outbound contract needs; there is no accessor for it
    beyond reading the file."""
    return json.loads((SCHEMA_RECORDS_DIR / f"{stage}.schema.json").read_text())


def schema_id_version(schema: dict) -> int:
    """The trailing integer of a schema's $id (".../records/build/2" -> 2)
    -- the single place a record shape's version lives. write_record reads
    this at call time rather than carrying its own copy, so the version it
    stamps cannot drift from what the schema declares (issue #60)."""
    return int(schema["$id"].rsplit("/", 1)[-1])


class RecordItemMismatch(RuntimeError):
    """A record's item field disagrees with the directory it was read
    from (issue #60, acceptance 5) -- a misfiled or copied record, refused
    rather than trusted."""


def _load_record(path: Path, item_id: str) -> dict:
    """The one place in this repository that turns a record file's bytes
    into a dict (issue #60, acceptance 6). Defaults record_version to 1
    when absent -- version 1 is every record written before this release
    and is never migrated -- and fills item from the directory when
    absent; refuses when a present item disagrees with it."""
    data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    data.setdefault("record_version", 1)
    item = data.get("item")
    if item is None:
        data["item"] = item_id
    elif item != item_id:
        raise RecordItemMismatch(
            f"{path}: item {item!r} disagrees with its directory {item_id!r}")
    return data


def parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict[int, dict]:
    attempts: dict[int, dict] = {}
    if not records_dir.is_dir():
        return attempts
    for f in records_dir.glob(f"{stage}-*.json"):
        m = RECORD_RE.match(f.name)
        if not m or m.group(1) != stage:
            continue
        try:
            attempts[int(m.group(2))] = _load_record(f, records_dir.name)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            continue
    return attempts


def latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict | None:
    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)
    return attempts[max(attempts)] if attempts else None


def next_attempt_number(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> int:
    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)
    return (max(attempts) + 1) if attempts else 1


def latest_attempt_path(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Path | None:
    """The path of the highest-numbered <stage>-NN.json, or None."""
    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)
    if not attempts:
        return None
    return records_dir / f"{stage}-{max(attempts):02d}.json"


def write_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str, record: dict, m: dict, item: str,
                 log: str | None = None) -> Path:
    """Every record gains record_version (this stage schema's own $id
    version -- issue #60) and item (verbatim from the caller, matching the
    record's directory segment), plus product_version (what ran, derived
    here) and product_pin (what the tenant declared, copied verbatim from
    the manifest, or null when the manifest carries no [attavo] table) --
    the latter two recorded, never enforced (issue #49). A manifest
    missing the table is an absent fact, not a crash: this is the one
    caller of write_record that must not raise on a shape the manifest
    schema forbids but tomllib.load happily returns.

    Refuses (raises ValueError) a record carrying a key this stage's
    schema does not declare -- the declared key set enforced with the
    standard library, not a jsonschema import: bin/attavo is a single
    script a tenant pins, and a runtime dependency changes installation.

    Also refuses a `status: "failed"` record that carries no trail at all
    -- log the empty or missing text it will sit beside, and refusal and
    halted (where the stage's schema has them) both null (issue #53).
    `returned`, `pass`, `halted`, and `refused` owe no such trail. log is
    None for a stage that invoked no adapter (verify's `returned`), which
    write_record then writes no log file for; otherwise the log is
    written before the record, so a refusal leaves no half-pair."""
    records_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    nn = next_attempt_number(records_dir, stage)
    schema = stage_schema(stage)
    record = {**record, "record_version": schema_id_version(schema), "item": item,
              "product_version": product_version(),
              "product_pin": (m.get("attavo") or {}).get("version")}
    undeclared = set(record) - set(schema.get("properties", {}))
    if undeclared:
        raise ValueError(f"write_record: {stage} record carries key(s) its schema does not "
                         f"declare: {', '.join(sorted(undeclared))}")
    if (record.get("status") == "failed" and not log
            and record.get("refusal") is None and record.get("halted") is None):
        raise ValueError(f"write_record: {stage} record status is 'failed' with no log, "
                         f"refusal, or halted to say why")
    path = records_dir / f"{stage}-{nn:02d}.json"
    if log is not None:
        path.with_suffix(".log").write_text(log)
    path.write_text(json.dumps(record, indent=2) + "\n")
    return path


def load_records(records_dir: Path) -> dict[str, dict[str, dict]]:
    """{item_id: {stage: latest_record}} for every <records>/<item>/<stage>-NN.json."""
    items: dict[str, dict[str, dict]] = {}
    if not records_dir.is_dir():
        return items
    for item_dir in sorted(p for p in records_dir.iterdir() if p.is_dir()):
        by_stage: dict[str, dict[int, dict]] = {}
        for rec in sorted(item_dir.glob("*.json")):
            m = RECORD_RE.match(rec.name)
            if not m:
                continue
            stage, n = m.group(1), int(m.group(2))
            try:
                data = _load_record(rec, item_dir.name)
            except json.JSONDecodeError:
                continue
            by_stage.setdefault(stage, {})[n] = data
        items[item_dir.name] = {stage: attempts[max(attempts)] for stage, attempts in by_stage.items()}
    return items


# --- doctor's three proofs ---

def proof_every_gate_rejects(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("every-gate-rejects", "every gate rejects its fixture")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no gate is registered")
    gates = m.get("gates") or {}
    if not gates:
        return p.fail("[gates] is empty; a gate that has rejected nothing does not exist")
    fixtures = root / m.get("paths", {}).get("rejections", "")
    # Proposal, not yet ruled on (issue #54): a gate that retires nothing
    # only adds, so proof one reports it here rather than waiting on the
    # schema alone -- read_manifest never validates against the manifest
    # schema at runtime, so a schema requirement is not an enforced one.
    no_retires = [name for name, gate in sorted(gates.items()) if not gate.get("retires")]
    missing, accepted = [], []
    for name, gate in sorted(gates.items()):
        fixture = fixtures / name
        if not fixture.is_dir():
            missing.append(name)
            continue
        env = dict(os.environ)
        item = fixture_item_id(fixture)
        if item is not None:
            env["ATTAVO_ITEM"] = item
        run = subprocess.run(gate["command"], shell=True, cwd=str(fixture),
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, env=env)
        if run.returncode == 0:
            accepted.append(name)
    if missing:
        return p.fail(f"gate(s) with no rejection fixture: {', '.join(missing)}")
    if accepted:
        return p.fail(f"gate(s) that accepted their rejection fixture: {', '.join(accepted)}")
    if no_retires:
        return p.fail(f"gate(s) with no retires: {', '.join(no_retires)}")
    return p.hold(f"{len(gates)} gate(s) rejected their fixture")


def merge_introducing_records(root: Path, records_rel: str, item: str,
                              stages: tuple[str, ...]) -> str | None:
    """The merge commit reachable from HEAD whose first-parent diff
    introduces a record for every one of stages, under
    records_rel/item/ -- the same derivation attest itself uses
    (derive_attest_items), read here so proof two requires an item's
    stage records to have arrived by an actual merge, not merely to sit
    in the tree (issue #69, acceptance row 4)."""
    try:
        merges = git(root, "log", "--merges", "--format=%H", "HEAD").split()
    except RuntimeError:
        return None
    prefix = f"{records_rel.rstrip('/')}/{item}/"
    for sha in merges:
        try:
            diff = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", f"{sha}^1", sha).splitlines()
        except RuntimeError:
            continue
        found: set[str] = set()
        for p in diff:
            if p.startswith(prefix):
                m = RECORD_RE.match(Path(p).name)
                if m:
                    found.add(m.group(1))
        if all(s in found for s in stages):
            return sha
    return None


def proof_one_item_every_stage(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("one-item-every-stage", "one item has traversed every stage")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no records directory is declared")
    records_rel = m.get("paths", {}).get("records", "")
    records_dir = root / records_rel
    items = load_records(records_dir)
    if not items:
        return p.fail(f"no work item under {records_dir.relative_to(root)}")
    complete = [item for item, stages in items.items() if all(s in stages for s in STAGES)]
    if not complete:
        best = max(items.items(), key=lambda kv: len(kv[1]))
        lacking = [s for s in STAGES if s not in best[1]]
        return p.fail(f"no item carries every stage; item {best[0]} lacks {', '.join(lacking)}")

    # issue #74: a complete item is counted only when its chain holds --
    # attavo attest refuses a build re-approved after the fact or a
    # verify whose build went stale, and the coherence gate refuses the
    # merge group on exactly that; a proof that never asked would call a
    # traversal the gate itself would not let through. The refusal text
    # is check_attest_chain's own, never re-typed here.
    refused, held, unmerged = [], [], []
    for item in complete:
        chain, message = check_attest_chain(records_dir / item, item)
        if chain is None:
            refused.append(f"item {item}: {message}")
            continue
        merge_sha = merge_introducing_records(root, records_rel, item, STAGES)
        if merge_sha:
            held.append((item, merge_sha))
        else:
            unmerged.append(item)

    if held:
        names = ", ".join(item for item, _ in held)
        shas = ", ".join(sha[:12] for _, sha in held)
        return p.hold(f"item(s) {names} carry {', '.join(STAGES)} with the chain held, "
                      f"merged at {shas}")
    if refused:
        return p.fail("; ".join(refused))
    return p.fail(f"item(s) {', '.join(unmerged)} carry every stage with the chain held but no "
                  f"merge commit's first-parent diff introduces all of them")


def touches_boundary(root: Path, sha: str, boundary_paths: list[str]) -> bool:
    """Whether sha's first-parent diff touches a path [spec.boundary].paths
    declares governed -- an ordinary merge that does is a boundary
    evasion (issue #72): the tenant carried a governed change around the
    path the manifest itself names."""
    diff = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", f"{sha}^1", sha).splitlines()
    return any(fnmatch.fnmatch(path, pattern) for path in diff for pattern in boundary_paths)


def proof_no_commit_lacks_record(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("no-commit-lacks-record", "no commit lacks a record")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; the tenant has no record boundary")
    try:
        install = git(root, "log", "--diff-filter=A", "--format=%H",
                      "--reverse", "--", MANIFEST).split()
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        return p.fail(f"git: {exc}")
    if not install:
        return p.fail(f"{MANIFEST} is not committed; the tenant has no record boundary")
    install_sha = install[0]
    full_chain = git(root, "rev-list", f"{install_sha}..HEAD", "--first-parent").split()
    if not full_chain:
        return p.fail(f"no commit after the install commit {install_sha[:12]}; nothing has been recorded")

    # issue #74: [doctor] from lets a tenant declare the doctor's read to
    # start at a later commit on main's first-parent line -- the point
    # after which every push arrived through the queue -- rather than the
    # install commit, which on a repository that adopted the product
    # after its history began is a boundary the doctor cannot otherwise
    # see. Any spelling git resolves is accepted (a short sha, a tag, a
    # ref) -- resolved with rev-parse --verify before the first-parent
    # check runs, so the two failure modes stay distinct: a spelling that
    # names no commit at all, and a commit that names one off the line.
    # Checked, not trusted: neither is silently forgiven back to install.
    declared_from = (m.get("doctor") or {}).get("from")
    window_start = install_sha
    if declared_from is not None:
        try:
            resolved_from = git(root, "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{declared_from}^{{commit}}").strip()
        except RuntimeError:
            return p.fail(f"[doctor] from {declared_from} does not resolve to a commit")
        if resolved_from != install_sha and resolved_from not in full_chain:
            return p.fail(f"[doctor] from {declared_from} is not on main's first-parent "
                          f"line after the install commit {install_sha[:12]}")
        window_start = resolved_from

    if window_start == install_sha:
        window_spec = f"{install_sha}..HEAD"
        commits = full_chain
    else:
        window_spec = f"{window_start}^..HEAD"
        commits = git(root, "rev-list", window_spec, "--first-parent").split()
    before_window = len(full_chain) - len(commits)

    records_rel = m.get("paths", {}).get("records", "")
    records_dir = root / records_rel
    named: set[str] = set()
    for stages in load_records(records_dir).values():
        for rec in stages.values():
            sha = rec.get("sha")
            if isinstance(sha, str):
                named.add(sha)
            for c in rec.get("commits", []) or []:
                if isinstance(c, str):
                    named.add(c)
    record_commits = set(git(root, "rev-list", window_spec, "--first-parent",
                             "--", records_rel).split()) if records_rel else set()
    merges = set(git(root, "rev-list", window_spec, "--first-parent", "--merges").split())
    boundary_paths = ((m.get("spec") or {}).get("boundary") or {}).get("paths") or []

    # Every first-parent commit in the window is exactly one of three
    # (issue #72): recorded (a record names it, or its diff touches
    # paths.records), ordinary (a merge that doesn't), or unrecorded
    # (anything else -- on a protected main with a queue, a non-merge
    # commit is the only way a change arrives without the forge).
    recorded, ordinary, unrecorded = [], [], []
    boundary_evasions = 0
    for c in commits:
        if c in named or c in record_commits:
            recorded.append(c)
        elif c in merges:
            ordinary.append(c)
            if boundary_paths and touches_boundary(root, c, boundary_paths):
                boundary_evasions += 1
        else:
            unrecorded.append(c)

    counts = f"{len(recorded)} recorded, {len(ordinary)} ordinary, {len(unrecorded)} unrecorded"
    evasions = boundary_evasions if boundary_paths else "n/a"
    extra = {"recorded": len(recorded), "ordinary": len(ordinary),
             "unrecorded": len(unrecorded), "boundary_evasions": evasions,
             "before_window": before_window}
    if unrecorded:
        return p.fail(f"{unrecorded[-1][:12]} lacks a record; {counts}; boundary evasions: "
                      f"{evasions}; before the window: {before_window}", **extra)
    return p.hold(f"{counts}; boundary evasions: {evasions}; before the window: {before_window}",
                  **extra)


def doctor(root: Path) -> list[Proof]:
    m = read_manifest(root)
    return [proof_every_gate_rejects(root, m),
            proof_one_item_every_stage(root, m),
            proof_no_commit_lacks_record(root, m)]


def cmd_doctor(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    as_json = "--json" in argv
    args = [a for a in argv if a != "--json"]
    root = Path(args[0] if args else ".").resolve()
    proofs = doctor(root)
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps({"root": str(root), "done": all(p.holds for p in proofs),
                          "proofs": [p.as_dict() for p in proofs]}, indent=2))
    else:
        for i, p in enumerate(proofs, 1):
            print(f"proof {i} {p.key}: {'HOLDS' if p.holds else 'FAILS'}: {p.reason}")
        print("doctor: done" if all(p.holds for p in proofs)
              else f"doctor: not done ({sum(not p.holds for p in proofs)} of 3 proofs fail)")
    return 0 if all(p.holds for p in proofs) else 1


# --- the spec contract ---

def parse_spec(spec_md: Path) -> tuple[dict | None, str | None]:
    lines = spec_md.read_text().splitlines()
    heading_idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith("## ")]
    headings = [lines[i].rstrip() for i in heading_idx]
    expected = ["## Problem", "## Claims", "## Criteria"]
    if headings != expected:
        bad = next((h for h in headings if h not in expected), None)
        if bad is None:
            missing = [h for h in expected if h not in headings]
            bad = missing[0] if missing else "heading order"
        return None, f"headings: found {headings or ['(none)']}, expected {expected} ({bad})"
    idx_problem, idx_claims, idx_criteria = heading_idx
    claims = [l.strip()[2:].strip() for l in lines[idx_claims + 1:idx_criteria]
              if l.strip().startswith("- ")]
    criteria = []
    for l in lines[idx_criteria + 1:]:
        s = l.strip()
        if not s.startswith("- ") or ":" not in s:
            continue
        name = s[2:].split(":", 1)[0].strip()
        criteria.append(name)
    return {"claims": claims, "criteria": criteria}, None


def run_criterion(exe: Path, cwd: Path) -> int:
    try:
        run = subprocess.run([str(exe)], cwd=str(cwd), capture_output=True,
                             timeout=CRITERION_TIMEOUT, check=False)
        return run.returncode
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124
    except OSError:
        return 126


def run_criteria(root: Path, spec_dir: Path, names: list[str]) -> list[dict]:
    criteria_dir = spec_dir / "criteria"
    stub_dir = spec_dir / "stub"
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        tree = Path(tmp) / "tree"
        stubbed = Path(tmp) / "stubbed"
        shutil.copytree(root, tree, ignore=shutil.ignore_patterns(".git"))
        shutil.copytree(root, stubbed, ignore=shutil.ignore_patterns(".git"))
        if stub_dir.is_dir():
            shutil.copytree(stub_dir, stubbed, dirs_exist_ok=True)
        results = []
        for name in names:
            exe = criteria_dir / name
            results.append({"name": name,
                            "on_tree": run_criterion(exe, tree),
                            "on_stub": run_criterion(exe, stubbed)})
        return results


def commit_postdates(root: Path, earlier: str, later: str) -> bool:
    """Whether later is a strict descendant of earlier on this branch."""
    if earlier == later:
        return False
    run = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", earlier, later],
                         capture_output=True, check=False)
    return run.returncode == 0


def cmd_spec_gate(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo spec-gate: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    spec_dir = root / specs_rel / item_id
    spec_md = spec_dir / "spec.md"
    if not spec_md.is_file():
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: no spec at {spec_dir}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    spec_rel = str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id)
    try:
        spec_sha = last_commit_touching(root, spec_rel)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    try:
        approved = approval_status(root, m, item_id) == "valid"
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, "approval")
    if approved:
        record = {"sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(), "spec_sha": spec_sha,
                  "at": now_iso(), "status": "approved",
                  "approval_sha": latest_approval.get("sha") if latest_approval else None,
                  "criteria": [], "refusal": None}
        write_record(records_dir, "spec", record, m, item_id)
        print("attavo spec-gate: approved (no criteria run)")
        return 0

    # Third verdict, beside "approved" and Phase 1's "pass": the approval
    # is not valid, but a build already ran after it -- the amendment that
    # expired the approval landed above the line, not below it, and the
    # tree the build produced is what must satisfy the criteria now, not
    # the stub that only ever proved they were reachable before approval.
    latest_build = latest_record(records_dir, "build")
    built = bool(
        latest_approval and latest_build and latest_build.get("status") != "halted"
        and commit_postdates(root, latest_approval["sha"], latest_build["sha"]))

    parsed, refusal = parse_spec(spec_md)
    results: list[dict] = []
    if refusal is None:
        if built:
            # Per criterion, not the set: passing on the tree is what the
            # build already satisfied; failing on the tree but passing on
            # the stub is a new obligation the next build must meet -- an
            # operator scripting a ruling the executor skipped. Refused
            # only when a criterion satisfies neither.
            results = run_criteria(root, spec_dir, parsed["criteria"])
            fail = next((c for c in results if c["on_tree"] != 0 and c["on_stub"] != 0), None)
            if fail:
                refusal = f"criterion fails against the tree plus stub: {fail['name']}"
        elif not parsed["claims"]:
            refusal = "claims: empty; the build may touch nothing"
        else:
            criteria_dir = spec_dir / "criteria"
            listed = parsed["criteria"]
            existing = sorted(p.name for p in criteria_dir.iterdir()) if criteria_dir.is_dir() else []
            prose = None
            for name in listed:
                if name not in existing:
                    prose = name
                    break
            if prose is None:
                for name in existing:
                    if name not in listed:
                        prose = name
                        break
            if prose is None:
                for name in listed:
                    if not os.access(criteria_dir / name, os.X_OK):
                        prose = name
                        break
            if prose:
                refusal = f"criterion is prose: {prose}"
            else:
                results = run_criteria(root, spec_dir, listed)
                fail_tree = next((c for c in results if c["on_tree"] == 0), None)
                if fail_tree:
                    refusal = f"criterion passes against the tree: {fail_tree['name']}"
                else:
                    fail_stub = next((c for c in results if c["on_stub"] != 0), None)
                    if fail_stub:
                        refusal = f"criterion fails against the tree plus stub: {fail_stub['name']}"

    status = "refused" if refusal else ("built" if built else "pass")
    record = {"sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(),
              "spec_sha": spec_sha,
              "at": now_iso(), "status": status, "approval_sha": None,
              "criteria": results, "refusal": refusal}
    write_record(records_dir, "spec", record, m, item_id)

    if refusal:
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: refused: {refusal}")
        return 1
    print(f"attavo spec-gate: {status} ({len(results)} criterion/criteria)")
    return 0


# --- approval, bound to a commit ---

def cmd_approve(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    by = None
    positional: list[str] = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        if argv[i] == "--by":
            by = argv[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(argv) else None
            i += 2
        else:
            positional.append(argv[i])
            i += 1
    item_id = positional[0] if positional else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo approve: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if not by:
        print("attavo approve: --by LOGIN is required", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo approve: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    spec_rel = str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id)
    try:
        spec_sha_now = last_commit_touching(root, spec_rel)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo approve: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    latest_spec = latest_record(records_dir, "spec")
    approvers = ((m.get("operators") or {}).get("approvers")) or []

    refusal = None
    if latest_spec is None or latest_spec.get("status") not in ("pass", "built"):
        refusal = f"no passing spec-gate record for item {item_id}"
    elif latest_spec.get("spec_sha") != spec_sha_now:
        refusal = (f"spec-gate record is stale: recorded {latest_spec.get('spec_sha')}, "
                   f"spec now at {spec_sha_now}")
    elif by not in approvers:
        refusal = f"{by} is not in [operators].approvers"

    if refusal:
        print(f"attavo approve: refused: {refusal}")
        return 1

    record = {"sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(), "spec_sha": spec_sha_now,
              "approved_by": by, "at": now_iso()}
    write_record(records_dir, "approval", record, m, item_id)
    print(f"attavo approve: item {item_id} approved by {by}")
    return 0


def approval_status(root: Path, m: dict, item_id: str) -> str:
    """"absent", "expired", or "valid": whether the latest approval record
    for item_id still binds to the spec's current commit. Raises
    ShallowCheckoutError on a shallow checkout, same as last_commit_touching.
    Shared by cmd_approval_status and cmd_build so build refuses on exactly
    what `attavo approval-status` would print.
    """
    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    spec_rel = str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id)
    spec_sha_now = last_commit_touching(root, spec_rel)
    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, "approval")

    if latest_approval is None:
        return "absent"
    if latest_approval.get("spec_sha") == spec_sha_now:
        return "valid"
    return "expired"


def cmd_approval_status(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo approval-status: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo approval-status: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    try:
        status = approval_status(root, m, item_id)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo approval-status: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    print(status)
    return 0 if status == "valid" else 1


# --- build: adapter, claims boundary, criteria, and the tenant's tests ---

STAGE_NAMES = ("build", "verify")


def merge_adapter_config(m: dict, adapter_name: str, stage: str) -> dict:
    """[executor.<adapter>] merged with [executor.<adapter>.<stage>], the
    stage table shadowing the parent for that stage only. Both build and
    verify call this before setting ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG, and the
    record's adapter_config is the merged result."""
    parent = (m.get("executor") or {}).get(adapter_name) or {}
    if not isinstance(parent, dict):
        return {}
    merged = {k: v for k, v in parent.items() if k not in STAGE_NAMES}
    stage_table = parent.get(stage)
    if isinstance(stage_table, dict):
        merged.update(stage_table)
    return merged


def resolve_adapter(root: Path, name: str) -> Path | None:
    """adapters/<name>/run, resolved first under the tenant's own
    attavo/adapters/, then under this product's adapters/."""
    for base in (root / "attavo" / "adapters", PRODUCT_ROOT / "adapters"):
        candidate = base / name / "run"
        if candidate.is_file():
            return candidate
    return None


def adapter_reports(adapter_path: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Field names adapters/<name>/conformance.json declares this adapter
    can supply with a real value -- vendor independence (2026-09-09
    ruling): the record carries the declaration, so an adapter claiming
    a field and returning null for it fails the conformance suite
    instead of writing a silent, uncomparable null. [] when the
    resolved adapter (a tenant override included) declares no
    conformance.json at all -- an absent declaration, not a refusal."""
    manifest_path = adapter_path.parent / "conformance.json"
    if not manifest_path.is_file():
        return []
    try:
        manifest = json.loads(manifest_path.read_text())
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return []
    return manifest.get("reports", [])


def changed_paths(root: Path, start_sha: str, records_rel: str) -> list[str]:
    """Paths changed between start_sha and HEAD, other than paths.records --
    the records directory is where build itself writes, not a claim."""
    out = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", start_sha, "HEAD").splitlines()
    prefix = records_rel.rstrip("/") + "/"
    return [p for p in out if p and p != records_rel and not p.startswith(prefix)]


def matches_a_claim(path: str, claims: list[str]) -> bool:
    return any(fnmatch.fnmatch(path, pattern) for pattern in claims)


def executor_identity(adapter_name: str) -> dict:
    """The executor's identity (issue #33): set by the product, never the
    tenant's git config, so an executor commit is never indistinguishable
    from an operator's own -- the specimen is attavo/code-factory@e22fa85.
    A reserved, undeliverable domain (.invalid) so no mailbox is implied.
    Distinct from the records app identity: the app says the product
    pushed this, this says an invocation authored this -- different
    facts, neither borrows the other's name."""
    return {"name": f"attavo executor ({adapter_name})",
            "email": f"executor+{adapter_name}@attavo.invalid"}


def commits_since(root: Path, start_sha: str) -> list[dict]:
    """(sha, name, email) for every commit after start_sha, oldest first."""
    out = git(root, "log", "--format=%H\x1f%an\x1f%ae", f"{start_sha}..HEAD")
    commits = []
    for line in out.splitlines():
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        sha, name, email = line.split("\x1f")
        commits.append({"sha": sha, "name": name, "email": email})
    return commits


def split_adapter_stdout(stdout: str) -> tuple[str, dict | None]:
    """(transcript, payload): the adapter's last non-empty stdout line is
    its JSON report, and everything before it is transcript, only when
    that last line actually parses as JSON. When it does not -- a tool's
    own error line printed last on a non-zero exit (issue #86: tenant
    zero attest/records/659/verify-01.log, codex's own "Input exceeds..."
    line, dropped because it was mistaken for an attempted payload) --
    nothing here is a report, and the whole stdout is the transcript, so
    that last line is not silently lost.
    """
    lines = stdout.splitlines()
    non_empty = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.strip()]
    if not non_empty:
        return stdout, None
    last = non_empty[-1]
    try:
        payload = json.loads(lines[last])
    except ValueError:
        return stdout, None
    transcript = "\n".join(lines[:last])
    return transcript, payload


def cmd_build(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo build: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo build: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    adapter_name = (m.get("executor") or {}).get("build")
    if not adapter_name:
        print("attavo build: refused: no [executor].build in the manifest")
        return 1

    tests_cfg = m.get("tests") or {}
    if not tests_cfg.get("select") or not tests_cfg.get("run"):
        print("attavo build: refused: no [tests] table in the manifest "
              "([tests].select and [tests].run are both required)")
        return 1

    try:
        status = approval_status(root, m, item_id)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo build: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if status != "valid":
        print(f"attavo build: refused: approval is {status}")
        return 1

    adapter_path = resolve_adapter(root, adapter_name)
    if adapter_path is None:
        print(f"attavo build: refused: no adapter named {adapter_name!r}")
        return 1

    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    spec_dir = root / specs_rel / item_id
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    spec_sha = last_commit_touching(root, str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id))
    latest_approval = latest_record(item_records_dir, "approval")
    parsed, _ = parse_spec(spec_dir / "spec.md")
    claims = parsed["claims"] if parsed else []
    criteria_names = parsed["criteria"] if parsed else []
    adapter_config = merge_adapter_config(m, adapter_name, "build")
    checks_command = (m.get("checks") or {}).get("command")
    identity = executor_identity(adapter_name)
    reports = adapter_reports(adapter_path)

    start_sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
    branch = git(root, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD").strip()
    started = time.monotonic()

    adapter_version = model = model_version = None
    tokens = {"input": 0, "output": 0}
    cost_usd = None
    transcripts: list[str] = []
    criteria_results: list[dict] = []
    selected_tests: dict | None = None
    checks_result: dict | None = None
    status_out = "failed"
    refusal = None
    halted = None
    iterations_used = 0

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        feedback_path = Path(tmp) / "feedback.txt"
        feedback_path.write_text("")

        for iteration in range(1, BUILD_ITERATION_BUDGET + 1):
            iterations_used = iteration
            env = dict(os.environ)
            env["ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR"] = str(spec_dir)
            env["ATTAVO_RECORDS_DIR"] = str(item_records_dir)
            env["ATTAVO_RULINGS"] = str(root / m["paths"]["rulings"])
            env["ATTAVO_BRANCH"] = branch
            env["ATTAVO_ITERATION"] = str(iteration)
            env["ATTAVO_FEEDBACK"] = str(feedback_path)
            env["ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG"] = json.dumps(adapter_config)
            env["ATTAVO_STAGE"] = "build"
            if checks_command:
                env["ATTAVO_CHECKS_COMMAND"] = checks_command
            env["GIT_AUTHOR_NAME"] = identity["name"]
            env["GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL"] = identity["email"]
            env["GIT_COMMITTER_NAME"] = identity["name"]
            env["GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL"] = identity["email"]

            run = subprocess.run([str(adapter_path)], cwd=str(root), env=env,
                                 capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
            transcript, payload = split_adapter_stdout(run.stdout)
            if run.returncode != 0:
                # The guarantee behind issue #53's refusal: an adapter that
                # exits non-zero always leaves a non-empty log, even one
                # that printed nothing at all on stderr.
                detail = f"[adapter exited {run.returncode}]"
                if run.stderr.strip():
                    detail += "\n" + run.stderr.rstrip("\n")
                transcript = (transcript + "\n" if transcript else "") + detail
            transcripts.append(transcript)

            if run.returncode != 0 or payload is None or not payload.get("model"):
                status_out = "failed"
                break

            adapter_version = payload.get("adapter_version")
            model = payload.get("model")
            model_version = payload.get("model_version")
            tok = payload.get("tokens") or {}
            tokens["input"] += tok.get("input", 0) or 0
            tokens["output"] += tok.get("output", 0) or 0
            iter_cost = payload.get("cost_usd")
            if iter_cost is not None:
                cost_usd = (cost_usd or 0) + iter_cost

            if payload.get("status") == "question":
                status_out = "halted"
                halted = {"question": payload.get("question")}
                break

            foreign = next((c for c in commits_since(root, start_sha)
                           if c["name"] != identity["name"] or c["email"] != identity["email"]),
                          None)
            if foreign:
                status_out = "refused"
                refusal = f"commit {foreign['sha']} not authored by the executor"
                break

            touched = sorted(changed_paths(root, start_sha, records_rel))
            bad_path = next((p for p in touched if not matches_a_claim(p, claims)), None)
            if bad_path:
                status_out = "refused"
                refusal = bad_path
                break

            criteria_results = [
                {"name": name, "exit": run_criterion(spec_dir / "criteria" / name, root)}
                for name in criteria_names]

            select_in = "".join(p + "\n" for p in touched)
            select_run = subprocess.run(tests_cfg["select"], shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                                        input=select_in, capture_output=True,
                                        text=True, check=False)
            identifiers = [l for l in select_run.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()]
            run_in = "".join(i + "\n" for i in identifiers)
            test_run = subprocess.run(tests_cfg["run"], shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                                      input=run_in, capture_output=True,
                                      text=True, check=False)
            selected_tests = {"select": tests_cfg["select"], "run": tests_cfg["run"],
                              "count": len(identifiers), "exit": test_run.returncode}

            checks_run = None
            if checks_command:
                checks_run = subprocess.run(checks_command, shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                                            capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
                checks_result = {"command": checks_command, "exit": checks_run.returncode}

            failing = [c for c in criteria_results if c["exit"] != 0]
            checks_ok = checks_run is None or checks_run.returncode == 0
            if not failing and test_run.returncode == 0 and checks_ok:
                status_out = "pass"
                break

            if iteration == BUILD_ITERATION_BUDGET:
                status_out = "failed"
                break

            feedback_lines = []
            if failing:
                feedback_lines.append("criteria failing:")
                feedback_lines += [f"- {c['name']}: exit {c['exit']}" for c in failing]
            if test_run.returncode != 0:
                feedback_lines += [f"test run exit {test_run.returncode}",
                                   "--- stdout ---", test_run.stdout,
                                   "--- stderr ---", test_run.stderr]
            if checks_run is not None and checks_run.returncode != 0:
                tail = (checks_run.stdout.splitlines() + checks_run.stderr.splitlines())
                tail = tail[-CHECKS_FEEDBACK_TAIL_LINES:]
                feedback_lines += [f"checks exit {checks_run.returncode}",
                                   "--- checks output (tail) ---", *tail]
            feedback_path.write_text("\n".join(feedback_lines) + "\n")

    record = {
        "sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(),
        "start_sha": start_sha,
        "spec_sha": spec_sha,
        "approval_sha": latest_approval.get("sha") if latest_approval else None,
        "at": now_iso(),
        "adapter": adapter_name,
        "adapter_config": adapter_config,
        "executor_identity": identity,
        "reports": reports,
        "adapter_version": adapter_version,
        "model": model,
        "model_version": model_version,
        "iterations": iterations_used,
        "criteria": criteria_results,
        "selected_tests": selected_tests,
        "checks": checks_result,
        "tokens": tokens,
        "cost_usd": cost_usd,
        "wall_clock_s": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),
        "status": status_out,
        "refusal": refusal,
        "halted": halted,
    }
    record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, "build", record, m, item_id,
                               log="\n".join(transcripts))

    print(f"attavo build: {status_out} ({record_path.name})")
    if status_out == "halted":
        return BUILD_HALT_EXIT
    return 0 if status_out == "pass" else 1


# --- verify: the forge's own read first, a judge only once it is green ---

FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS = {"success", "neutral", "skipped"}


def read_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> dict:
    """Runs the tenant's [forge].checks_command on sha; the product does
    not know what forge that command reads. One line per check as
    "<name> <conclusion>"; a command that fails to run, or any conclusion
    other than success/neutral/skipped, is not green."""
    run = subprocess.run(f"{checks_command} {shlex.quote(sha)}", shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    conclusions = []
    for line in run.stdout.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        parts = line.rsplit(" ", 1)
        if len(parts) == 2:
            conclusions.append({"name": parts[0], "conclusion": parts[1]})
    # A commit the forge has no record of is not green -- it is unread.
    # The executor's own commit is exactly this: the forge attaches
    # checks to a pull request's head, and that commit stops being the
    # head the moment a record commit lands on top of it. all() over an
    # empty list is True, which is why this is spelled out rather than
    # implied by the loop above.
    ok = run.returncode == 0 and bool(conclusions) and all(
        c["conclusion"] in FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS for c in conclusions)
    return {"command": checks_command, "sha": sha, "conclusions": conclusions}, ok


def cmd_verify(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo verify: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo verify: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id

    latest_build = latest_record(item_records_dir, "build")
    if latest_build is None:
        print(f"attavo verify: refused: no build record for item {item_id}")
        return 1
    if latest_build.get("status") != "pass":
        print(f"attavo verify: refused: latest build record is "
              f"{latest_build.get('status')!r}, not pass")
        return 1

    forge_cfg = m.get("forge") or {}
    checks_command = forge_cfg.get("checks_command")
    if not checks_command:
        print("attavo verify: refused: no [forge].checks_command in the manifest")
        return 1

    build_sha = latest_build["sha"]
    build_record_path = latest_attempt_path(item_records_dir, "build")
    started = time.monotonic()
    head_sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
    # The forge attaches checks to a pull request's head, not to the
    # executor's own commit -- that commit stops being the head the
    # moment a record commit lands on top of it (issue #44). Reading the
    # forge on head_sha is what the forge actually checked and what will
    # be merged; build_sha and forge_checks["sha"] can differ, and both
    # are kept on the record rather than one sha serving two purposes.
    forge_checks, forge_ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, head_sha)

    if not forge_ok:
        record = {
            "sha": head_sha,
            "build_sha": build_sha,
            "build_record": build_record_path.stem if build_record_path else None,
            "approval_sha": latest_build.get("approval_sha"),
            "at": now_iso(),
            "adapter": None, "adapter_config": {}, "adapter_version": None,
            "model": None, "model_version": None,
            "reports": [],
            "forge_checks": forge_checks,
            "findings": None,
            "tests_named": [],
            "commits": [],
            "mutation": None, "weak_stub": False, "stub_ratio": None,
            "tokens": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "cost_usd": None,
            "wall_clock_s": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),
            "status": "returned",
        }
        record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, "verify", record, m, item_id)
        print(f"attavo verify: returned ({record_path.name}); the forge checks are not green")
        return 0

    executor = m.get("executor") or {}
    adapter_name = executor.get("verify") or executor.get("build")
    if not adapter_name:
        print("attavo verify: refused: no [executor].verify or [executor].build in the manifest")
        return 1
    adapter_path = resolve_adapter(root, adapter_name)
    if adapter_path is None:
        print(f"attavo verify: refused: no adapter named {adapter_name!r}")
        return 1

    adapter_config = merge_adapter_config(m, adapter_name, "verify")
    reports = adapter_reports(adapter_path)
    approval_sha = latest_build.get("approval_sha")
    spec_dir = root / specs_rel / item_id
    branch = git(root, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD").strip()

    # The item's own work since approval, not everything merged onto the
    # branch since then (issue #52): a three-dot diff against approval_sha
    # itself is not enough, since approval_sha is already an ancestor of
    # HEAD in the normal flow and merge-base(approval_sha, HEAD) is just
    # approval_sha again -- verified empirically, see the issue comment.
    # Diffing from the merge-base of HEAD and the remote's default branch
    # instead excludes content that arrived by merging that branch in,
    # because that content is already present on both sides of the merge
    # base. refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is what names the default branch
    # without hard-coding "main"; when it is not set, there is nothing
    # to diff against and this refuses rather than guess.
    diff_text = ""
    if approval_sha:
        symref = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", str(root), "symbolic-ref", "-q", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        if symref.returncode != 0:
            print("attavo verify: refused: refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is not set; "
                  "run `git remote set-head origin -a`")
            return 1
        diff_base = git(root, "merge-base", symref.stdout.strip(), "HEAD").strip()
        diff_text = git(root, "diff", diff_base, "HEAD")

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        diff_path = Path(tmp) / "diff.patch"
        diff_path.write_text(diff_text)

        env = dict(os.environ)
        env["ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR"] = str(spec_dir)
        env["ATTAVO_RECORDS_DIR"] = str(item_records_dir)
        env["ATTAVO_RULINGS"] = str(root / m["paths"]["rulings"])
        env["ATTAVO_BRANCH"] = branch
        env["ATTAVO_STAGE"] = "verify"
        env["ATTAVO_DIFF"] = str(diff_path)
        env["ATTAVO_BUILD_RECORD"] = str(build_record_path) if build_record_path else ""
        env["ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG"] = json.dumps(adapter_config)

        run = subprocess.run([str(adapter_path)], cwd=str(root), env=env,
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        transcript, payload = split_adapter_stdout(run.stdout)
        if run.returncode != 0:
            # Same guarantee as cmd_build's, issue #53: a non-zero adapter
            # exit always leaves a non-empty log.
            detail = f"[adapter exited {run.returncode}]"
            if run.stderr.strip():
                detail += "\n" + run.stderr.rstrip("\n")
            transcript = (transcript + "\n" if transcript else "") + detail

    # Verify is a report, not a commit (issue #46): the adapter is invoked
    # on the branch head it was handed, and must leave that head alone.
    # Checked by comparing the head before the invocation (head_sha,
    # already read above for the forge) against the head after it.
    post_adapter_head = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
    commits: list[str] = []
    if post_adapter_head != head_sha:
        commits = git(root, "rev-list", f"{head_sha}..{post_adapter_head}", "--reverse").split()

    status_out = "failed"
    findings = None
    tests_named: list[str] = []
    model = model_version = adapter_version_out = None
    tokens = {"input": 0, "output": 0}
    cost_usd = None

    if run.returncode == 0 and payload is not None and payload.get("model"):
        model = payload.get("model")
        model_version = payload.get("model_version")
        adapter_version_out = payload.get("adapter_version")
        tok = payload.get("tokens") or {}
        tokens = {"input": tok.get("input", 0) or 0, "output": tok.get("output", 0) or 0}
        cost_usd = payload.get("cost_usd")
        # "findings" absent (or explicitly null) is not the same as an
        # empty list: the adapter had no channel to report one at all,
        # which is not a clean bill of health (issue #46). A commit
        # during verify fails the attempt regardless of what it reported.
        if not commits and "findings" in payload and payload["findings"] is not None:
            findings = payload["findings"]
            tests_named = payload.get("tests_named") or []
            status_out = "pass"

    record = {
        "sha": head_sha,
        "build_sha": build_sha,
        "build_record": build_record_path.stem if build_record_path else None,
        "approval_sha": approval_sha,
        "at": now_iso(),
        "adapter": adapter_name,
        "adapter_config": adapter_config,
        "adapter_version": adapter_version_out,
        "model": model,
        "model_version": model_version,
        "reports": reports,
        "forge_checks": forge_checks,
        "findings": findings,
        "tests_named": tests_named,
        "commits": commits,
        "mutation": None, "weak_stub": False, "stub_ratio": None,
        "tokens": tokens,
        "cost_usd": cost_usd,
        "wall_clock_s": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),
        "status": status_out,
    }
    record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, "verify", record, m, item_id, log=transcript)

    print(f"attavo verify: {status_out} ({record_path.name})")
    return 0 if status_out == "pass" else 1


# --- file-findings: one tenant issue per finding, nothing filed silently ---

def cmd_file_findings(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo file-findings: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo file-findings: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id

    forge_cfg = m.get("forge") or {}
    file_issue_command = forge_cfg.get("file_issue_command")
    if not file_issue_command:
        print("attavo file-findings: refused: no [forge].file_issue_command in the manifest")
        return 1

    latest_verify = latest_record(item_records_dir, "verify")
    if latest_verify is None:
        print(f"attavo file-findings: refused: no verify record for item {item_id}")
        return 1

    findings = latest_verify.get("findings") or []
    if not findings:
        print("attavo file-findings: no findings to file")
        return 0

    for finding in findings:
        if not finding.get("retires"):
            title = finding.get("title", "<untitled>")
            print(f"attavo file-findings: refused: finding {title!r} carries no retires; "
                  f"nothing filed")
            return 1

    for finding in findings:
        run = subprocess.run(file_issue_command, shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                             input=json.dumps(finding) + "\n", capture_output=True,
                             text=True, check=False)
        if run.returncode != 0:
            title = finding.get("title", "<untitled>")
            print(f"attavo file-findings: refused: filing {title!r} exited {run.returncode}: "
                  f"{run.stderr.strip()}")
            return 1

    print(f"attavo file-findings: filed {len(findings)} issue(s)")
    return 0


# --- record-only: whether HEAD, against its first parent, touches only
# paths.records. Core logic about a git checkout, not a forge's shell
# (issue #19); a workflow template's push-and-retrigger guard is one line
# calling this instead of carrying its own copy. ---

def cmd_record_only(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo record-only: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    try:
        changed = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", "HEAD^1", "HEAD").splitlines()
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        print(f"attavo record-only: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    prefix = records_rel.rstrip("/") + "/"
    only_records = all(p == records_rel or p.startswith(prefix) for p in changed if p)
    print("attavo record-only: yes" if only_records else "attavo record-only: no")
    return 0 if only_records else 1


# --- attest: the signed envelope over one item's whole traversal ---

def derive_attest_items(root: Path, records_rel: str) -> tuple[set[str], str | None]:
    """The item ids from the merge commit's own first-parent diff -- record
    paths under records_rel/<N>/, not a branch name, which a squash merge
    discards and which names a forge's convention the product does not
    know. Returns (items, None), else (empty set, reason) only when the
    diff itself could not be read -- zero items found is not an error
    here (issue #58): not every merge is an item merge, and the caller
    decides what zero, one, or more than one means."""
    try:
        changed = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", "HEAD^1", "HEAD").splitlines()
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        return set(), f"git: {exc}"
    prefix = records_rel.rstrip("/") + "/"
    items: set[str] = set()
    for p in changed:
        if p.startswith(prefix):
            rest = p[len(prefix):]
            item = rest.split("/", 1)[0]
            if item:
                items.add(item)
    return items, None


NOT_YET = object()


def check_attest_chain(records_dir: Path, item_id: str, partial: bool = False):
    """The three assertions attavo attest refuses on: the latest spec,
    approval, build, and verify records for item_id all exist, build's
    approval_sha names the latest approval -- a re-approval after a build
    must force a re-run of what changed -- and verify's build_sha names
    that build. Returns (None, message) on any of them, the message being
    the same text attest prints, not a copy of it a caller re-types
    (issue #74: proof two reuses it); returns ((spec, approval, build,
    verify), None) when the chain holds.

    partial (issue #67) is for a pull request, where a later stage's
    record cannot exist yet because the stage that would produce it is
    itself waiting on this check. Under partial, a missing record is not
    a refusal -- the two chain assertions still run over whatever records
    exist, and (NOT_YET, message) is returned, the message naming the
    item and the stages present and absent, when a stage is still missing
    once those assertions hold."""
    latest_spec = latest_record(records_dir, "spec")
    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, "approval")
    latest_build = latest_record(records_dir, "build")
    latest_verify = latest_record(records_dir, "verify")
    stages = (("spec", latest_spec), ("approval", latest_approval),
              ("build", latest_build), ("verify", latest_verify))

    if not partial:
        for stage, rec in stages:
            if rec is None:
                return None, f"no {stage} record for item {item_id}"

    if latest_approval is not None and latest_build is not None:
        if latest_build.get("approval_sha") != latest_approval.get("sha"):
            return None, ("build's approval_sha does not name the latest approval; a "
                          "re-approval after the build must force a re-run")
    if latest_build is not None and latest_verify is not None:
        if latest_verify.get("build_sha") != latest_build.get("sha"):
            return None, "verify's build_sha does not name the latest build"

    missing = [stage for stage, rec in stages if rec is None]
    if partial and missing:
        present = [stage for stage, rec in stages if rec is not None]
        return NOT_YET, (f"item {item_id} not yet (present: {', '.join(present) or 'none'}; "
                         f"absent: {', '.join(missing)})")

    return (latest_spec, latest_approval, latest_build, latest_verify), None


def cmd_attest(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    partial = "--partial" in argv
    positional = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("--")]
    unknown = [a for a in argv if a.startswith("--") and a != "--partial"]
    if unknown:
        print(f"attavo attest: unknown argument {unknown[0]!r}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    root = Path(".").resolve()
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo attest: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_id = positional[0] if positional else None
    if item_id is None:
        items, err = derive_attest_items(root, records_rel)
        if err:
            print(f"attavo attest: refused: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        # Not every merge is an item merge -- a merge that carries no item
        # at all is an ordinary fact, not a defect (issue #58); the
        # attest template runs this on every push and has nothing to
        # sign or commit when nothing was attested, and its existing
        # `if:` guards already read this step's own exit code.
        if not items:
            print("attavo attest: no item derived from the merge commit's record paths; "
                  "nothing to attest")
            return 0
        if len(items) > 1:
            print(f"attavo attest: refused: {len(items)} items derived from the merge "
                  f"commit's record paths: {', '.join(sorted(items))}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        item_id = next(iter(items))

    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    chain, message = check_attest_chain(records_dir, item_id, partial=partial)
    if chain is None:
        print(f"attavo attest: refused: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if chain is NOT_YET:
        print(f"attavo attest --partial: {message}")
        return 0
    latest_spec, latest_approval, latest_build, latest_verify = chain

    spec_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "spec")
    approval_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "approval")
    build_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "build")
    verify_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "verify")

    # sha and merge_sha are the same commit here -- the one this statement
    # was computed at. On the merge group that commit is the one main will
    # hold; kept as two fields because a forge-specific template signs
    # this statement against "the commit that lands", naming it merge_sha
    # is what makes that unambiguous to a reader who has never seen attest
    # run anywhere but the merge group.
    head_sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()

    statement = {
        "sha": head_sha,
        "merge_sha": head_sha,
        "item": item_id,
        "spec": spec_path.stem if spec_path else None,
        "spec_sha": latest_spec.get("sha"),
        "approval": approval_path.stem if approval_path else None,
        "approval_sha": latest_approval.get("sha"),
        "build": build_path.stem if build_path else None,
        "build_sha": latest_build.get("sha"),
        "verify": verify_path.stem if verify_path else None,
        "verify_sha": latest_verify.get("sha"),
        "at": now_iso(),
        # Neither is populated by anything today -- no ruling or amendment
        # log exists yet for either to read from. Carried as empty rather
        # than omitted, since the schema names them.
        "interventions": [],
        "acts": [],
        "tokens_by_stage": {"build": latest_build.get("tokens"), "verify": latest_verify.get("tokens")},
        # Never summed across stages -- the 2026-09-09 ruling on reporting
        # across vendors: a stage's executor may report no cost at all.
        "cost_by_stage_usd": {"build": latest_build.get("cost_usd"), "verify": latest_verify.get("cost_usd")},
        "wall_clock_by_stage_s": {"build": latest_build.get("wall_clock_s"),
                                  "verify": latest_verify.get("wall_clock_s")},
        # The product does not count a forge's own CI runs; a template
        # that wants this reported sets ATTAVO_CI_RUNS from its own read.
        "ci_runs": int(os.environ.get("ATTAVO_CI_RUNS") or 0),
        "product_version": product_version(),
        "product_pin": (m.get("attavo") or {}).get("version"),
    }
    print(json.dumps(statement, indent=2))
    return 0


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0 if argv else 2
    if argv[0] == "doctor":
        return cmd_doctor(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "spec-gate":
        return cmd_spec_gate(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "approve":
        return cmd_approve(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "approval-status":
        return cmd_approval_status(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "build":
        return cmd_build(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "verify":
        return cmd_verify(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "file-findings":
        return cmd_file_findings(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "record-only":
        return cmd_record_only(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "attest":
        return cmd_attest(argv[1:])
    print(f"attavo: unknown subcommand {argv[0]!r}; only doctor, spec-gate, approve, "
          f"approval-status, build, verify, file-findings, record-only, attest exist "
          f"at this commit", file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
